 FII DII 
 Foreign institutional investors (FIIs) were net sellers of Rs 2,977.86 crore in the cash market on September 15, while domestic institutional investors (DIIs) were net buyers of Rs 2,686.05 crore, as Indian equities fell sharply amid rising crude oil prices and bond yields.
FIIs bought shares worth Rs 13,194.76 crore and sold shares worth Rs 16,172.62 crore during the session. DIIs bought Rs 15,221.98 crore and sold Rs 12,535.93 crore, according to exchange data.
With Tuesday's flows, FIIs have turned net sellers of Rs 2,399.11 crore in September so far, while DIIs have invested Rs 27,673.01 crore in the month.
On a year-to-date basis, FIIs have sold a net Rs 3.61 lakh crore from the cash market, while DIIs have invested Rs 5.91 lakh crore.
Indian equities ended sharply lower on Tuesday, with the Nifty falling 1.19% to 23,118.60 and the Sensex declining 1.04% to 74,003.82, as rising crude prices and the US 10-year Treasury yield heightened inflation and interest-rate concerns.
The sell-off broadened through the session, with financials, auto and other rate-sensitive sectors coming under pressure. Mid- and small-cap stocks fell more sharply than the benchmark indices, while IT stocks offered some support, with the Nifty IT index gaining around 2.2%.
Sunny Agrawal, Deputy Vice President – Fundamental Research, SBI Securities, said Indian equities ended a volatile session on a weak note as investors turned cautious amid rising crude oil and bond yields, persistent inflation concerns and uncertainty ahead of key global central bank decisions.
“Broad-based selling across financials, real estate, industrial stocks weighed on benchmark indices through the latter half of the session,” Agrawal said.
IT was the only meaningful pocket of strength, supported by gains in major export-oriented technology companies. Banking and other rate-sensitive segments saw profit booking as investors assessed the risk that elevated energy prices could keep inflationary pressures high and limit the scope for near-term policy easing.
Agrawal also said the recent uptick in wholesale inflation had reinforced concerns over elevated cost pressures, while heightened volatility reflected increased investor nervousness ahead of a busy week of global monetary policy announcements.
