The United States has sought fresh details from India on the use of government-held rice for ethanol production, bringing the country's expanding grain-based biofuel programme back into scrutiny at the World Trade Organization.
The US has asked how India is complying with the WTO's Bali decision on public stockholding while continuing to procure rice at administered prices and release part of those stocks for non-food uses, including ethanol, the Economic Times reported on September 16. Washington has also sought a breakdown of central and state government expenditure on public stockholding, foodgrain distribution and sales to industrial users such as ethanol manufacturers, according to the report.
The questions are expected to be taken up at the WTO Committee on Agriculture meeting later this month. The WTO has scheduled its next regular Agriculture Committee meeting for September 24-25.
The issue links two separate Indian policies: government procurement of rice for food security and the push to blend ethanol into petrol.
India procures paddy at minimum support prices and maintains rice stocks in the Central Pool for requirements under the National Food Security Act and other welfare programmes. Surplus stocks can also be released under the Open Market Sale Scheme, or OMSS.
In August 2024, the Department of Food and Public Distribution allowed ethanol distilleries to participate in FCI rice auctions again. Its August 29 order initially permitted distilleries with ethanol allocations from oil marketing companies to lift a maximum of 23 lakh metric tonnes of rice between August and October 2024.
The government subsequently expanded the programme. In February 2025, it allowed ethanol distilleries to buy as much as 24 LMT of FCI rice at a fixed price of Rs 2,250 per quintal under the OMSS.
By August 2025, the Petroleum Ministry said the government had approved 52 LMT of surplus FCI rice for ethanol production for Ethanol Supply Year 2024-25 and another 52 LMT for 2025-26, up to June 30, 2026.
The role of FCI rice in ethanol production has also grown. The Petroleum Ministry said in July 2026 that FCI rice accounted for 24.64 percent of ethanol production in ESY 2025-26, compared with 0.02 percent in 2023-24.
 Why does India have rice available for ethanol? 
Government stocks have remained substantially above prescribed buffer norms.
As of May 28, 2026, rice stocks in the Central Pool stood at 397 LMT, compared with the July 1 buffer norm of 135.40 LMT, according to the Department of Food and Public Distribution. Around 298 LMT of procured paddy was also awaiting milling and addition to rice stocks.
The government's position is that ethanol does not take precedence over food distribution. In a July 31 statement, the Petroleum Ministry said FCI rice is approved for ethanol only after requirements under the Public Distribution System, the National Food Security Act, welfare schemes and mandatory buffer stocks have been met.
This policy sits alongside India's broader ethanol programme. The government advanced its target of blending 20 percent ethanol into petrol from 2030 to ESY 2025-26. Average blending reached 20 percent during November 2025-June 2026, according to the Petroleum Ministry.
The dispute is rooted in WTO rules governing agricultural support.
Under the WTO Agreement on Agriculture, developing countries generally have a de minimis threshold allowing product-specific support of up to 10% of the value of production before it counts towards limits on trade-distorting domestic support.
India's rice support has exceeded that threshold. In its 2024-25 notification to the WTO, India said it had exceeded the de minimis limit for rice and invoked the protection available under the 2013 Bali Ministerial Decision on public stockholding for food-security purposes.
The Bali mechanism, commonly called the 'peace clause', protects qualifying public stockholding programmes of developing countries from certain WTO dispute-settlement challenges when subsidy limits are breached, provided specified conditions are met. A 2014 WTO decision extended that mechanism until members agree on a permanent solution.
One of the conditions is that stocks procured under such programmes should not distort trade or adversely affect the food security of other WTO members. Countries relying on the protection must also meet notification and transparency requirements.
 This dispute predates the latest US questions 
India's rice support was already under examination at the WTO before the latest development.
At the May 27-28 Committee on Agriculture meeting, the US, Australia, Paraguay and Ukraine submitted a counter-notification challenging India's calculations of market price support for rice and wheat. They argued that the support was higher than India had notified and was affecting global markets.
India rejected those calculations, telling the WTO that the underlying data were disputed and that its policies were necessary for food security and consistent with WTO rules.
